---

<Base
  title="How this course works"
  description="The new way DIGHT 250 and DIGHT 340 work from Oct 19: the loop, the assignments, your repo, and the AI policy."
>
  <PageMeta courses={['dight250', 'dight340']} />
  <h1>How this course works</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    From Oct 19, this course works a new way. AI coding tools can now write most of the syntax we’ve been
    practicing. So from here on, you’ll learn what professional developers actually do with them: decide what to
    build, direct the tool, check its work, and explain every choice.
  </p>
  <p class="setup">
    Here for setup? Go to Spark 0 for <a href="/250/spark-0/">DIGHT 250</a> ·
    <a href="/340/spark-0/">DIGHT 340</a>.
  </p>

  <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
    <h2 id="toc-heading">On this page</h2>
    <ol>
      {toc.map((t) => <li><a href={`#${t.id}`}>{t.label}</a></li>)}
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <!-- ===================================================================== -->
  <section aria-labelledby="why">
    <h2 id="why">Why we’re changing</h2>
    <p>
      You won’t learn less. The month you’ve spent writing code by hand is exactly what lets you read and judge what an
      AI produces. We’ll build more, and you’ll understand it better.
    </p>
    <p>Here’s how it works now:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>You read before you delegate.</strong> You’ll hand more of the writing to the agent as we cover each
        concept, but only once you can read what it writes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>You’re accountable for every line.</strong> You must be able to explain anything you submit. Small,
        frequent checks show you understand it; the code alone doesn’t.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>We build first, then dig deeper.</strong> Making things comes first. Docs links and the
        <a href="/skills/dig-deeper/"><code>/dig-deeper</code></a> skill are there whenever you want to slow down.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Plain tools.</strong> One public repo per course, pushed to <code>main</code>, live on GitHub Pages.
        Learning Suite is just for links and grades.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Free tools.</strong> Nothing in this course requires a paid account. DIGHT 340 uses Claude Pro if you
        have it, with Copilot Free as the fallback.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Each class teaches one concept, usually with a live build where the agent gets something wrong and we catch it
      together. The rest of class is studio time: you build, and I come round.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================================================================== -->
  <section aria-labelledby="loop">
    <h2 id="loop">The loop</h2>
    <p>Every Spark, Prototype, and Release follows the same five steps.</p>

    <ol class="loop">
      {
        loop.map((l) => (
          <li class={l.who === 'Agent' ? 'loop-agent' : undefined}>
            <span class="loop-step">{l.step}</span>
            <span class="loop-who">{l.who}</span>
            <span class="loop-text">{l.text}</span>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>

    <details>
      <summary>Predict: of the five steps, how many does the agent do?</summary>
      <p>
        One: <strong>Generate</strong>. The other four are yours. You decide what to build and what “done” means, you
        check the result, and you explain it. That’s the part of the job the agent can’t do for you, and it’s what this
        course teaches.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================================================================== -->
  <section aria-labelledby="assignments">
    <h2 id="assignments">Sparks, Prototypes, and the Release</h2>
    <p>
      The names tell a product story: an idea (Spark), a working model (Prototype), then something shipped to real users
      (Release).
    </p>

    <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Assignment tiers">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Tier</th>
            <th scope="col">What it is</th>
            <th scope="col">When</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><Badge kind="spark" /></th>
            <td>15–30 minutes, one concept.</td>
            <td>One every class, started in studio time. Due before the next class.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><Badge kind="prototype" /></th>
            <td>Combines the week’s concepts into one piece.</td>
            <td>Weekly from Oct 26, due the following Monday. Four in all.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><Badge kind="release" /></th>
            <td>Your final project, for a real or realistic audience. Use the default brief or choose your own.</td>
            <td>Brief out Nov 16; replaces Prototypes for the last few weeks.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      You’ll also write two more reflections (and an optional third), 200–300 words each, like the ones you’ve already
      done. Dates are in your course’s section below.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================================================================== -->
  <section aria-labelledby="grading">
    <h2 id="grading">How it’s graded</h2>
    <p>
      Each piece is checked against a short list of what “done” means. It either meets the list or it’s “Not yet,” and
      “Not yet” means fix it and resubmit.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Sparks: Complete or Not yet.</strong> Complete means the folder exists, its
        <a href="/notes/"><code>NOTES.md</code></a> covers every section, and the live page loads. Sparks are graded
        straight from your repo; there’s nothing to submit.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Prototypes: Exceeds, Meets, or Not yet.</strong> Each brief comes with its “done” list, starting with P1
        on Oct 26. You submit two links in Learning Suite: your live page and the code.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Release: a rubric plus a walkthrough.</strong> It covers functionality, code quality, Git, AI use (your
        <code>NOTES.md</code>), accessibility, whether it solved the problem you set out to solve, and a 5-minute
        walkthrough with me at the final-exam open lab.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Reflections: pass or fail,</strong> as now. Written by you, without AI.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      <strong>No late penalties.</strong> Due dates are there to keep you on pace. Rework is welcome until the cutoffs:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Wed Oct 28</strong> for work from before Oct 19 (moved from Oct 21).</li>
      <li><strong>Wed Dec 9</strong> for everything from Oct 19 on, except the Release.</li>
    </ul>

    <details>
      <summary>Predict: you finish a Spark two days after it’s due. What happens?</summary>
      <p>
        Nothing bad. There are no late penalties, so it counts as Complete once it meets the list. Just don’t let them
        pile up: each Spark sets up the next class, and the Prototypes build on them.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================================================================== -->
  <section aria-labelledby="repo">
    <h2 id="repo">Your repo</h2>
    <p>
      You keep one public repo per course and push straight to <code>main</code>. No pull requests, no classroom
      platform. Spark 0 reorganizes the repo you already have into this layout:
    </p>

    <pre>sparks/s01-repo-restructure/   one folder per Spark
prototypes/p01-…/              one folder per Prototype
release/                       your final project
archive/                       everything from before Oct 19
index.html                     your hub, linking to all of it
starter/NOTES.md               the NOTES.md template
AGENTS.md                      the course rules for the agent</pre>

    <p>
      Every assignment folder has its own <code>NOTES.md</code>. The kit also adds the slash commands:
      <code>.github/skills/</code> in 250, <code>.claude/</code> in 340.
    </p>

    <details>
      <summary>Predict: after Spark 0, do the links to your old assignments still work?</summary>
      <p>
        No. Your old work moves into <code>archive/</code>, so its address changes: in 250,
        <code>…github.io/cartoon/</code> becomes <code>…github.io/archive/cartoon/</code>. In 340 the repo rename changes
        every link too: <code>…github.io/dight340-exercises/ex03/</code> becomes
        <code>…github.io/dight340/archive/ex03/</code>. The pages still work at their new address. If you’re reworking an old assignment before Oct 28, resubmit its new link in Learning Suite. A
        resubmission just means “new URL”; say so in the comment if it’s also ready to regrade.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================================================================== -->
  <section aria-labelledby="ai-policy">
    <h2 id="ai-policy">AI policy and NOTES.md</h2>
    <p>
      From Oct 19, <strong>AI agents are expected for all code</strong> in this course. This replaces the old “no
      AI-written code” rule and <code>ai-notes.md</code>.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The agent works within the course’s rules, set by the <a href="/agents/"><code>AGENTS.md</code></a> file in
        your repo.
      </li>
      <li>
        Every assignment folder has a <a href="/notes/"><code>NOTES.md</code></a>: your intent, the prompts you used,
        what you kept, changed, or rejected, one thing you dug into, and what you still don’t understand.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>You must be able to explain any line you submit.</strong> During studio time I’ll sometimes ask you to
        explain a line in 2 minutes. Those checks are ungraded practice. The Release walkthrough is the graded version.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Reflections stay human-written.</strong> No AI to draft, outline, or rewrite them.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout" role="note" aria-label="Your repo is public">
      <p>
        <strong>Your repo is public.</strong> Anyone can read your <code>NOTES.md</code>, including the “Open questions”
        section, where you write what you still don’t understand. That’s what it’s for, and I use it to choose
        questions for your checks and walkthrough. Just write about the code, and leave out anything personal. You can
        use a pseudonymous GitHub username if you prefer.
      </p>
    </div>

    <details>
      <summary>Predict: the agent wrote a line you can’t explain. Should you submit it?</summary>
      <p>
        Not yet. Highlight it and run <a href="/skills/dig-deeper/"><code>/dig-deeper</code></a>, read the MDN link,
        and change the line if you find a clearer way. Submit it when you could explain it to someone else.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Predict: can you ask the agent to fix the grammar in your reflection?</summary>
      <p>
        No. Reflections are written without AI: no drafting, outlining, or rewriting, and that includes tidying. The
        agent is told the same thing in <code>AGENTS.md</code>.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================================================================== -->
  <section class="start" aria-labelledby="start">
    <h2 id="start">Start here: pick your course</h2>
    <p>
      Each course has its own Spark 0 guide, skills, and details (agent, scope, repo, topics, Prototypes, Release).
      Spark 0 sets up your repo: download the starter kit, add it to your repo, and an agent walks you through the
      rest, one step at a time.
    </p>
    <CourseCards to="spark-0/" level={3} />
  </section>
</Base>

